<ol class="breathe-horizontal" start="1"> 


  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00291">arXiv:2611.00291</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.00291">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2611.00291">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2611.00291">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tags is-inline-block">
        <span class="tag is-small is-link tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Human-Computer Interaction">cs.HC</span>
        
          
            <span class="tag is-small is-grey tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Artificial Intelligence">cs.AI</span>
          
            <span class="tag is-small is-grey tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Computers and Society">cs.CY</span>
          
        </div>
      
    </div>
    
    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        When AI <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agents</span> Act on Our Behalf: A Taxonomy of Human-<span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> Interaction Failure
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Wang%2C+C">Canwen Wang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Xiao%2C+Q">Qing Xiao</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhu%2C+H">Haiyi Zhu</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Shen%2C+H">Hong Shen</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2611.00291v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        AI <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> write and&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2611.00291v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2611.00291v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2611.00291v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        AI <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> write and <span class="search-hit mathjax">execute</span> code, manipulate user interfaces, and <span class="search-hit mathjax">transact</span> on their users&#39; behalf. We ask what forms failure takes in human-<span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> interaction, and how those forms change as <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> act with greater autonomy. From 695 documented incidents in two public repositories, we developed 12 failure modes bottom-up within three interaction stages adapted from Amershi et al.&#39;s guidelines, coding the autonomy at which each arose. What a person can do when an <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> fails depends on how much the <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> completes between points of user involvement--a property fixed at deployment. Therefore, the same model on the same task can fail differently under different configurations. All 12 modes appear at both low and high autonomy, and rising autonomy changes the form each failure takes. Users need controls between full delegation and full manual work, and neither incident records nor <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> benchmarks reach the interaction where these failures occur.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2611.00291v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2611.00291v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 30 October, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> November 2026.
      
    </p>
    

    

    
  </li>

  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39733">arXiv:2610.39733</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39733">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2610.39733">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2610.39733">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tags is-inline-block">
        <span class="tag is-small is-link tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Artificial Intelligence">cs.AI</span>
        </div>
      
    </div>
    
    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Bank-World: A Benchmark for Evaluating <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agents</span> on Banking Workflows
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Rupawalla%2C+D">Daniel Rupawalla</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.39733v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Bank-World is a benchmark of 50 banking tasks across two simulated institutions. Completing banking work requires more than producing the right calculation: <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> must reconcile records, obtain permission,&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.39733v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.39733v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.39733v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Bank-World is a benchmark of 50 banking tasks across two simulated institutions. Completing banking work requires more than producing the right calculation: <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> must reconcile records, obtain permission, <span class="search-hit mathjax">execute</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">transactions</span>, and keep instructions and status reports consistent as circumstances change. Bank-World tests these requirements through single-turn assignments and multi-turn interactions with simulated employees who supply information, approve actions, and revise instructions. Grading combines <span class="search-hit mathjax">executable</span> checks of recorded actions and state with model judgments of task requirements, including whether valid alternative solutions satisfy them. Across 1,600 attempts by four models, we find that correct decisions often fail to become completed work. <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agents</span> charge fees twice, distribute the correct total in incorrect amounts among the recipients, and <span class="search-hit mathjax">execute</span> payments while approval is pending. Their strengths also differ: Sol leads single-turn completion, Opus has the highest observed multi-turn completion rate, and Grok succeeds across more multi-turn tasks but less consistently. Bank-World makes these differences inspectable through task-level results and worked examples connecting source evidence, <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> decisions, and simulated consequences. We retain the task environments, <span class="search-hit mathjax">executable</span> graders, and full trajectories privately to reduce contamination and preserve the benchmark for future evaluations.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.39733v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.39733v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 29 October, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2026.
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="comments is-size-7">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Comments:</span>
      <span class="has-text-grey-dark mathjax">43 pages, 3 figures, including appendices. Ancillary files contain the task ID mapping, reward specifications, and single-turn check-authority mapping</span>
    </p>
    

    

    
  </li>

  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39234">arXiv:2610.39234</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39234">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2610.39234">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2610.39234">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tags is-inline-block">
        <span class="tag is-small is-link tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Cryptography and Security">cs.CR</span>
        
          
            <span class="tag is-small is-grey tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Computation and Language">cs.CL</span>
          
        </div>
      
    </div>
    
    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        AgentHeal: Evaluating Goal-Preserving Resource Recovery of Web <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agents</span> under Access Barriers
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Liu%2C+Z">Zhiyi Liu</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Du%2C+H">Haohua Du</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Sun%2C+H">Haoqi Sun</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Tan%2C+H">Haisheng Tan</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.39234v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Large language model (LLM) <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> have demonstrated strong capabilities in automating complex information-seeking and&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.39234v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.39234v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.39234v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Large language model (LLM) <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> have demonstrated strong capabilities in automating complex information-seeking and <span class="search-hit mathjax">transactional</span> tasks across the open web. However, their real-world reliability remains limited by access barriers, such as dead links, authentication walls, rate limits, and anti-scraping defenses. These barriers block the primary <span class="search-hit mathjax">execution</span> path, causing <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> to abort prematurely, loop indefinitely, or quietly compromise the user&#39;s original constraints. Existing evaluations either implicitly assume that target resources are freely accessible or focus on transient perturbations that simple retries can resolve, failing to capture scenarios where <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> must actively reroute around impassable bottlenecks. To bridge this gap, this paper introduces AgentHeal, a comprehensive benchmark evaluating goal-preserving resource recovery, defined as the ability of web <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> to detect non-transient access barriers, pivot to legitimate alternative sources, and fulfill the user&#39;s exact original objective. Spanning nine barrier families across a counterfactual sandbox and real-website replay, our experiments on leading LLM backbones show that average task success drops sharply from 89.4% to 43.7% under access barriers. Further analysis reveals that <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> frequently fall for deceptive look-alikes, fail to escalate to users when necessary, violate access policies, or waste computation on futile retries, providing actionable diagnostics for developing resilient web <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span>.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.39234v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.39234v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 28 October, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2026.
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="comments is-size-7">
