      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Jiang%2C+D">Daxin Jiang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Bai%2C+Z">Zuo Bai</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.36980v2-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Large language model (LLM) <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> are extending&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.36980v2-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.36980v2-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.36980v2-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Large language model (LLM) <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> are extending <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>-mediated commerce on the Web from shopping for consumers to operating businesses for sellers. In online-to-offline (O2O) commerce, decisions are issued through Web-based platforms but realized through costly physical <span class="search-hit mathjax">execution</span>. Evaluating such <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> requires going beyond individual <span class="search-hit mathjax">transactions</span> to ask whether decisions made online turn into sustained economic returns. We introduce AutoVend, a benchmark in which one LLM <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> manages 20 vending machines across five site types through a Web-based operating interface for 180 simulated days. Its data-grounded simulator combines a shared warehouse, procurement lead times, paid replenishment, inventory expiration, and recurring operating costs. We evaluate five LLM <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> against a rule-based inventory-control baseline and diagnose their site coordination, <span class="search-hit mathjax">execution</span>, and performance over time. Four of five evaluated LLM <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> fall short of this baseline, and returns per dollar of inference cost rank models differently from returns alone. Operational analyses show that gains in individual metrics do not consistently yield stronger economic growth, as inventory and <span class="search-hit mathjax">execution</span> costs erode these gains. Trajectory analysis further reveals difficulties in grounding feasible actions, managing inventory commitments, and converting feedback into effective policy revisions.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.36980v2-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.36980v2-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 4 November, 2026; <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">v1</span> submitted 27 October, 2026;
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2026.
      
    </p>
    

    

    
