    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Provable Compliance: A Protocol for Runtime Compliance Evidence and <span class="search-hit mathjax">Transaction</span>-Specific Decisioning
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Blackburn%2C+J">Jeremy Blackburn</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Hines%2C+T">Tim Hines</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Koepp%2C+M">Matthew Koepp</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Lessard%2C+R">Russell Lessard</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Patrick%2C+J">Jason Patrick</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.22913v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        &hellip;onboarding, screening, monitoring, and review, but compliance evidence ages, and whether it supports a decision depends on the institution, product, subject, counterparty, <span class="search-hit mathjax">transaction</span>, jurisdiction, risk tier, and purpose. A relying party therefore often cannot obtain compact, attributable proof of which evidence, policy, authority, and&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.22913v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.22913v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.22913v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Regulated institutions already perform onboarding, screening, monitoring, and review, but compliance evidence ages, and whether it supports a decision depends on the institution, product, subject, counterparty, <span class="search-hit mathjax">transaction</span>, jurisdiction, risk tier, and purpose. A relying party therefore often cannot obtain compact, attributable proof of which evidence, policy, authority, and <span class="search-hit mathjax">transaction</span> context were current when a decision was made, a gap that grows as authority is delegated to software <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> and scheduled workflows. We describe ChainIT Provable Compliance, the compliance-side layer of the ChainIT Authority Protocol. Each sourced or institution-generated fact is represented as a separate Evidence Assertion VDT with provenance, freshness, confidence, permitted purpose, and privacy classification; a versioned Compliance Policy Profile selects the applicable rules; a Business Rules Engine returns deterministic, reason-coded predicate results; and the organization&#39;s configured Workflow Engine composes them into a signed, time-bounded Compliance Decision VDT for one reliance event. The decision contributes a single predicate, Compliance Decision Permits, to session-eligibility and <span class="search-hit mathjax">transaction</span>-policy decisions. It never independently authorizes payment: only a final Allow together with an exact <span class="search-hit mathjax">transaction</span> digest, atomic capacity reservation, and a single-use <span class="search-hit mathjax">execution</span> credential can commit value. The paper specifies point-of-reliance freshness, scoped propagation of evidence changes, decision states, evidence domains (AML, sanctions, PEP, background, identity, and licensing), the U.S. and FATF regulatory context as of August 2026, privacy and disclosure controls, and residual risks.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.22913v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.22913v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 16 October, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2026.
      
    </p>
    
