    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Provable Authority: A Protocol for Verifiable Authority Resolution and Control Across Human, Organizational, and <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agentic</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">Transactions</span>
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Blackburn%2C+J">Jeremy Blackburn</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Hines%2C+T">Tim Hines</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Koepp%2C+M">Matthew Koepp</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Lessard%2C+R">Russell Lessard</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Patrick%2C+J">Jason Patrick</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.22899v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Payment systems authenticate users, devices, accounts, and cryptographic credentials, but a relying party, such as a counterparty, payment rail, marketplace, or autonomous <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> runtime, generally cannot obtain compact, independently verifiable evidence that the party presenting one exact&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.22899v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.22899v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.22899v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Payment systems authenticate users, devices, accounts, and cryptographic credentials, but a relying party, such as a counterparty, payment rail, marketplace, or autonomous <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> runtime, generally cannot obtain compact, independently verifiable evidence that the party presenting one exact <span class="search-hit mathjax">transaction</span> holds current organizational authority to <span class="search-hit mathjax">execute</span> it under the applicable evidence, policy, budget, and approval state. <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agentic</span> commerce makes this gap operationally urgent: a software <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> can produce a valid signature while acting outside its delegated purpose, after its sponsor&#39;s authority has changed, or on <span class="search-hit mathjax">transaction</span> details that differ from those a human approved. We describe the ChainIT Authority Protocol, a subject-neutral and rail-neutral pre-<span class="search-hit mathjax">execution</span> authority and evidence control plane. The protocol separates durable identity, a versioned organizational authority record (the Authority Resolution Pactvera), sourced evidence assertions, deterministic reason-coded policy predicates, and organization-configured dispositions; binds each decision and human approval to a canonical <span class="search-hit mathjax">transaction</span> digest; reserves capacity atomically so that limits hold under concurrency; and authorizes commit only through a single-use, sender-constrained <span class="search-hit mathjax">Execution</span> Authorization Credential consumed at the rail adapter, with append-only lifecycle evidence sealed after the terminal outcome. An <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> Subject Profile adds the workload registration, attested key hierarchy, and sovereignty boundary required when the acting subject is an AI <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>, without introducing a separate authority concept. We specify the commit-time <span class="search-hit mathjax">execution</span> predicate and bounded-freshness status rules, give enforcement profiles for fiat (ACH, wire, card, instant payment) and stablecoin rails, and state residual risks and non-goals.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.22899v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.22899v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

